---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About & methodology" description="What Agentic Atlas is, who it's for, and how the ingestion pipeline keeps it current.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-14">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--color-fog-100)]">About Agentic Atlas</h1>
    <div class="prose-atlas prose prose-invert mt-8 max-w-none">
      <p>
        <strong>Agentic Atlas</strong> is an open, living technical reference for the people who
        actually ship AI-agent systems: software engineers and architects rolling agentic
        platforms into production. It sits somewhere between an <em>Awesome AI Agents</em> list
        and a focused news aggregator — but every entry is written to teach, compare, and warn.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <p>Content is tagged by level so it serves a wide spectrum of readers:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Beginner</strong> — you know software, but agents are new. Start in Foundations.</li>
        <li><strong>Intermediate</strong> — you've built a tool-using agent and want patterns and frameworks.</li>
        <li><strong>Advanced</strong> — you run multi-agent systems, swarms, and long-running harnesses, and you care about failure modes, evaluation, and cost.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How content is organized</h2>
      <p>
        Everything is Markdown, versioned in Git, under <code>src/content/</code>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>foundations/</code> — the pre-agentic era, LLM→agent evolution, agent anatomy, glossary</li>
        <li><code>frameworks/</code> — a structured catalog (LangChain, LangGraph, Claude Agent SDK, Strands, MCP, A2A, …)</li>
        <li><code>patterns/</code> — reflection, planning, orchestration, swarms, context engineering, RAG/GraphRAG</li>
        <li><code>production/</code> — harnesses, evaluation, observability, guardrails, cost, and failure modes</li>
        <li><code>comparisons/</code> — framework matrices and failure-mode/tradeoff tables</li>
        <li><code>case-studies/</code> — real deployments from public engineering blogs</li>
        <li><code>news/</code> — aggregated ecosystem developments</li>
        <li><code>learning-paths/</code> — curated sequences from beginner to architect</li>
        <li><code>lab/</code> — notes from our own lab: what each project shipped, what it cost, and the evidence behind the claim</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every article carries frontmatter with its level, tags, last-updated date, sources, and
        license. Articles are sized for a <strong>5–15 minute read</strong>; the build validates
        the frontmatter contract so nothing drifts.
      </p>

      <h2>How it stays current: the ingestion pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        The knowledge base extends itself. A developer maintains a list of reference links in
        <code>pipeline/sources.yaml</code>. On a schedule (and on demand) a GitHub Action runs
        <code>pipeline/ingest.py</code>, which:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Diffs</strong> the source list against <code>pipeline/registry.json</code> using content hashes, ETags, and declared versions — so already-processed material is skipped.</li>
        <li><strong>Fetches and extracts</strong> the readable content of anything new or changed, caching a snapshot.</li>
        <li><strong>Plans</strong> where each source belongs (section, level, tags) and what article(s) it should produce.</li>
        <li><strong>Drafts</strong> a 5–15 minute Markdown article with a full sources/attribution block. With an <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> present it uses a Claude model; without one it emits a structured outline stub for a human to finish.</li>
        <li><strong>Indexes</strong> the corpus and opens a pull request for review.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Nothing is auto-published without landing in Git first, so a human always reviews machine
        drafts. Machine-drafted pages are labeled until reviewed.
      </p>

      <h2>Sourcing &amp; licensing</h2>
      <p>
        Articles <em>summarize and link to</em> primary sources; they do not republish third-party
        text. Each references its sources with publisher and license. The site's own code is
        Apache 2.0 and its original prose is CC BY 4.0. Referenced material remains under its own
        license and copyright. See <a href="/licenses">Licenses &amp; attribution</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
